## Founding & History
Hartzview Vineyard was established in 1988 by Rob and Anthea Patterson. The vineyard began with the planting of its first Pinot Noir vines in 1988. Before becoming a vineyard, Hartzview was a thriving fruit farm in the early 1980s, known for producing a significant amount of Australia's gooseberries. The idea of planting grapevines was inspired by a family trip to Burgundy, France, where Rob and Anthea developed a passion for fortified wine production and recognized Tasmania's potential for fruit-based fortified wines. Site and soil testing revealed characteristics similar to Burgundy and Bordeaux, making it suitable for Pinot Noir cultivation.

## People
Hartzview Vineyard is a family-owned business, lovingly tended by Rob and Anthea Patterson. The Patterson family has run the vineyard since 1976. Their daughter, Edwina, has established Gardners Bay Distillery, which produces award-winning Eximious Gin and Raspberry Gin.

## Vineyard & Site
Hartzview Vineyard is situated in Gardners Bay, Tasmania, offering views across vineyard slopes, Port Cygnet, and the Hartz mountains. It is considered one of Australia's southernmost vineyards. The vineyard spans 3 hectares and was established on the site of an old gooseberry farm. The cool climate of the region contributes to the intense aromas and complex flavors of their wines. Estate-grown Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, and Chardonnay grapes are hand-picked.

## Cellar Door Experience
The cellar door at Hartzview Vineyard is open from 10 am to 4 pm, Monday to Saturday. Some sources indicate it is open until 5 pm daily. Visitors can enjoy tastings of estate-grown Pinot Noir, Sauvignon Blanc, and Chardonnay, as well as the range of preservative-free fruit fortified wines and liqueurs. Tastings also include Gardners Bay Distillery's Eximious Gin and Raspberry Gin. The cellar door offers panoramic views of the surrounding countryside. Guests can also take a self-guided stroll through the Heritage-listed pickers' huts.

## Food & Hospitality
Hartzview Vineyard features a café that offers a culinary experience with a menu focusing on fresh, seasonal, local Tasmanian produce. The café serves all-day lunches, specializing in vineyard and cheese platters, coffee, and desserts. Some sources indicate the café is currently closed, with products available online and accommodation for booking. However, other sources state the café is open every day from 10 am to 5 pm. Self-contained accommodation in a colonial-style homestead is available on the property, sleeping up to 7 people across three bedrooms.

## Awards & Recognition
The Patterson's daughter, Edwina, has established Gardners Bay Distillery, which produces an award-winning Eximious Gin. No reliable information found from public sources regarding specific awards or critic scores for Hartzview Vineyard wines.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Hartzview Vineyard has Sustainable Tourism Accreditation by ATIC. Their fortified wines are noted as being preservative-free, with no artificial flavors or colorings, and made from 100% high-quality Tasmanian fruit. Some wines are described as sustainably produced.

## Visiting Practical Info
Hartzview Vineyard is located at 70 Dillons Road, Gardners Bay, Tasmania, 7112. The phone number is (03) 6295 1623. The website is http://www.hartzview.com.au/. It is approximately a 45 to 60-minute drive south of Hobart. Visitors can also find Hartzview Vineyard at the Salamanca Market in Hobart on Saturdays at site 28. For winter hours or appointments, it is advisable to check their website, social media, or call.

## Notable / Distinctive
Hartzview Vineyard is notable for being one of Australia's southernmost vineyards. It is particularly distinctive for its extensive range of preservative-free fruit fortified wines and liqueurs, made from 100% high-quality Tasmanian fruit. The property also features Heritage-listed fruit pickers' huts, established in the 1920s, which visitors can explore on a self-guided walk. During World War Two, some of these huts housed Italian prisoners of war. The vineyard also offers spectacular views of the Hartz mountain ranges, the Huon River Valley, and Port Cygnet.

